{"test_cases_lookup_map": {"{\"actual_output\": \"Short answer: model evals test the model itself; application evals test the whole LLM-powered application. Below is a simple comparison by scope, goals, and metrics.\\n\\nScope\\n- Model evals: Focus on the LLM alone \\u2014 its capabilities on prepared datasets and benchmarks. Used when a new model is released to measure how the model performs in isolation.\\n- Application evals: Focus on the full LLM-based application or system \\u2014 the model plus retriever, tools, orchestration, deployment environment, and user flows.\\n\\nGoals\\n- Model evals: Measure and document the model\\u2019s raw capabilities, produce benchmark/leaderboard results, and compare models.\\n- Application evals: Judge whether the LLM-powered application meets practical needs in real use cases (end-to-end behavior and usefulness in the actual setup).\\n\\nMetrics (and testing setup)\\n- Model evals: Dataset-driven accuracy, benchmark scores and leaderboard percentages. The eval is the full testing setup (what dataset, when run, tools used), not just a single metric.\\n- Application evals: Application-specific measures \\u2014 e.g., retriever accuracy or other component-level scores and system-level outcomes determined by the app and its testing setup (tools like RAG, offline vs. production tests, etc.). Again, the eval includes the whole setup, not only metrics.\\n\\nNote: these two labels (model eval vs application eval) are informal terms used here to simplify the idea; in industry both fall under \\u201cLLM evals,\\u201d and people infer which type from the use case.\", \"context\": null, \"expected_output\": \"**Model evals vs. app evals in LLM testing**\\n\\n- **Scope**\\n  - **Model evals:** Evaluate the **LLM itself**.\\n  - **App evals:** Evaluate the **LLM-powered application** as a whole or its **components** like retriever, embedding model, or reranker.\\n\\n- **Goal**\\n  - **Model evals:** Check the model\\u2019s **capabilities, accuracy, and benchmark performance** when a new LLM is released.\\n  - **App evals:** Check whether the **product works properly** for the intended use case.\\n\\n- **Metrics**\\n  - **Model evals:** Benchmarks, leaderboards, accuracy, and capability tests.\\n  - **App evals:** Correctness of answer, faithfulness, hallucination, relevance, beginner-friendliness, latency, token cost, and component performance.\\n\\nIn short: **model evals test the model; app evals test the product built with the model.**\", \"hyperparameters\": null, \"input\": \"Compare model evals vs app evals in LLM testing: scope, goals, and metrics.\", \"retrieval_context\": null}": {"cached_metrics_data": [{"metric_data": {"name": "Answer Relevancy", "threshold": 0.5, "success": false, "strictMode": false, "flaky": false, "evaluationModel": "gpt-5.4", "evaluationCost": 0, "inputTokenCount": 0, "outputTokenCount": 0}, "metric_configuration": {"threshold": 0.5, "evaluation_model": "gpt-5.4", "strict_mode": false, "include_reason": true}}, {"metric_data": {"name": "simpleness [GEval]", "threshold": 0.5, "success": false, "strictMode": false, "flaky": false, "evaluationModel": "gpt-5.4", "evaluationCost": 0}, "metric_configuration": {"threshold": 0.5, "evaluation_model": "gpt-5.4", "strict_mode": false, "include_reason": false, "evaluation_params": ["input", "actual_output"]}}]}, "{\"actual_output\": \"Determinism\\n- Traditional ML systems are deterministic: same input -> same output (example: calculator 2+3 always =5).  \\n- LLM apps are probabilistic and non-deterministic: the same input can produce different, but valid, outputs. Outputs are semantically flexible (different words/paragraphs can all be correct). Simple assertion-based tests that expect exact strings will fail.\\n\\nDynamic cost\\n- Traditional ML: compute/cost is roughly fixed per data size. Example: 100 rows -> cost = 10 rupees when hosted on a server.  \\n- LLM apps: cost is dynamic and hard to fix because both input length and output length matter. You pay for input tokens and output tokens; evaluation itself is more costly (text outputs need detailed, often human, judgment).\\n\\nInput-output tracking\\n- Traditional ML: easy to track inputs to outputs; evaluation can be automated with mathematical formulas.  \\n- LLM apps: you must manage a changing relationship between input and output because nothing is static. Outputs are variable and semantically flexible, so you need richer evaluation (detailed reports, component-level checks). Multiple components (e.g., retriever and generator) can fail independently, so tracking must handle those separate failure modes. Human annotation doesn\\u2019t scale, pushing toward automated but more complex approaches.\", \"context\": null, \"expected_output\": \"ML systems are generally deterministic: the same input row gives the same output every time, with fixed compute/cost for that input size.  \\n\\nLLM apps are probabilistic and dynamic: the same input can produce different outputs, and both cost and compute vary because input length, output length, and retrieved context all change.  \\n\\nAlso, ML prediction usually has a simple input-to-output flow, while LLM apps often require tracking the full journey from input through retrieval, prompting, and output.\", \"hyperparameters\": null, \"input\": \"How do ML systems and LLM apps differ in determinism, dynamic cost, and input-output tracking?\", \"retrieval_context\": null}": {"cached_metrics_data": [{"metric_data": {"name": "Answer Relevancy", "threshold": 0.5, "success": false, "strictMode": false, "flaky": false, "evaluationModel": "gpt-5.4", "evaluationCost": 0, "inputTokenCount": 0, "outputTokenCount": 0}, "metric_configuration": {"threshold": 0.5, "evaluation_model": "gpt-5.4", "strict_mode": false, "include_reason": true}}, {"metric_data": {"name": "simpleness [GEval]", "threshold": 0.5, "success": false, "strictMode": false, "flaky": false, "evaluationModel": "gpt-5.4", "evaluationCost": 0}, "metric_configuration": {"threshold": 0.5, "evaluation_model": "gpt-5.4", "strict_mode": false, "include_reason": false, "evaluation_params": ["input", "actual_output"]}}]}, "{\"actual_output\": \"The generator can still hallucinate even when retrieval is correct because the LLM may ignore or not use the provided context and instead fabricate answers.\\n\\nMain causes (from the context):\\n- The generator (LLM) simply ignores the correct context and produces wrong content (hallucination).\\n- The model returns output in a different or incorrect format than you expect, so it appears wrong even though context was correct.\\n- Prompting injection / jailbreak: adversarial input can override guardrails and make the model follow harmful or wrong instructions.\\n- In agentic/autonomous setups, hallucinations can be \\u201csilent\\u201d (harder to detect) because the system acts without obvious wrong outputs for you to correct.\\n\\nThese are generator-side failures distinct from retriever failures, so you need a separate evaluation pipeline for the generator.\", \"context\": null, \"expected_output\": \"A generator can hallucinate even when retrieval is correct by ignoring the retrieved context and producing an answer from its own assumptions or from unrelated parts of the context. This happens because the retriever may fetch the right documents, but the generator can still misread them, over-prioritize certain text, or add unsupported facts.\", \"hyperparameters\": null, \"input\": \"How can a generator hallucinate despite correct retrieval, and what causes it?\", \"retrieval_context\": null}": {"cached_metrics_data": [{"metric_data": {"name": "Answer Relevancy", "threshold": 0.5, "success": false, "strictMode": false, "flaky": false, "evaluationModel": "gpt-5.4", "evaluationCost": 0, "inputTokenCount": 0, "outputTokenCount": 0}, "metric_configuration": {"threshold": 0.5, "evaluation_model": "gpt-5.4", "strict_mode": false, "include_reason": true}}, {"metric_data": {"name": "simpleness [GEval]", "threshold": 0.5, "success": false, "strictMode": false, "flaky": false, "evaluationModel": "gpt-5.4", "evaluationCost": 0}, "metric_configuration": {"threshold": 0.5, "evaluation_model": "gpt-5.4", "strict_mode": false, "include_reason": false, "evaluation_params": ["input", "actual_output"]}}]}}}